{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And upon the nobles of the children of Israel.</b> They are called 'nobles' (אצילי) from the root of 'emanation' (אצילות), meaning that a spirit of prophecy emanated upon them in this holy assembly. And these nobles were Nadav and Avihu and seventy of the elders of Israel." "<b>He did not</b> stretch forth His hand. From here, that they were liable to the stretching forth of a hand. <b>And they beheld the Elohim.</b> This is what was mentioned above: ״ויראו את אלהי ישראל״ — and the intention in this, that Scripture said ״ויראו את אלהי ישראל״ and ״ויחזו את האלהים,״ is that they beheld the Elohim and did not overreach to ascend toward HaShem. <b>And they ate and drank.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), they made a festive day because they merited and entered into that honored vision and were delivered and emerged in peace, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 32:31): ״כי ראיתי אלהים פנים אל פנים ותנצל נפשי״; and so too the High Priest would make a great feast on the eve following Yom HaKippurim, because he entered in peace and emerged in peace from the Holy of Holies and was delivered." "<b>And there are those who</b> explained ״ויאכלו וישתו״ to mean that their level was not like the level of Moses, concerning whom Scripture testified (Exodus 34:28) ״ויהי שם עם ה׳ ארבעים יום וארבעים לילה״ — and he was sustained for forty days without food and drink — for after they attained this rank, they immediately returned to their human level when they ate and drank." "<b>And Rashi</b> explained ״ויחזו את האלהים ויאכלו וישתו״ to mean that while they were eating and drinking they were gazing upon Him with a coarse heart." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> ״ויאכלו וישתו״ — did they then bring delicacies (קילור) up with them to Sinai? Thus they expounded in Vayikra Rabbah, parashat Acharei Mot. The meaning of קילור is a cake. Rather, ״ויאכלו וישתו״ — said Rabbi Yochanan: it was genuine eating, as is stated (Proverbs 16:15): ״באור פני מלך חיים.״ And this 'light,' since the nourishment and delight of the soul in that wondrous vision, Scripture calls 'eating and drinking,' which is the delight of the body. And so David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 63:6): ״כמו חלב ודשן תשבע נפשי,״ for since the body is sustained from physical nourishment that comes through cause after cause, how much more so is the soul sustained when it binds itself to the supreme first Cause, blessed be He — and He is the true, enduring nourishment, and the eating is the certain, eternal eating. And in the manner of what is written (Psalms 36:9–10): ״ירוין מדשן ביתך ונחל עדניך תשקם כי עמך מקור חיים באורך נראה אור.״" "<b>And furthermore,</b> one may interpret ויאכלו וישתו ('and they ate and drank') by reading it as וישיתו ('and they drank'), for since after the giving of the Torah we find in the punishment (Exodus 33:4): ״ולא שתו איש עדיו עליו״ — and no man put on his adornment — and the great reward and mighty splendor is not mentioned in the section of the giving of the Torah, therefore he needed to allude here that what they beheld of Elohim was that they put on that adornment (עדי) and took pleasure and rejoiced with the joy of the soul, like one who takes pleasure and rejoices in eating and drinking. This I heard from the mouth of the rabbi, R. Dan." "<b>And still</b> the question remains: why was this not mentioned explicitly at the giving of the Torah, and why does he not mention the matter of this adornment (עדי) when we merited it, yet he mentions it when it was lost from us? And it seems to me to say that it was not necessary at all, for the people who went forth from the camp to meet Elohim to receive Torah from heaven, and saw the voices and the torches and the sound of the shofar and all those great and awesome things that their eyes saw — there is no need to say that they clothed themselves in the adornment of light and were crowned and sanctified with the sanctity of the names of the angels, for everyone who draws near to the oil has no need to be told that he is anointed from it, as the Sages, blessed be their memory, said: 'He drew near to the oil, and it anointed him.'" "<b>And behold,</b> this is like the account of the World to Come, which is not mentioned in the Torah because there was no need, since all that the Torah lengthens in recounting the physical promises is only because they are a preparation to inherit the life of the World to Come. And therefore there was no need here to mention the adornment when we merited it, for since Scripture says (Exodus 19:17): ״ויוצא משה את העם לקראת האלהים״ — from that general statement one may infer the particular; but when they sinned in the matter of the calf, he needed to mention their lack, for this mighty distinction was lost from them." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״ויחזו את האלהים ויאכלו וישתו״ — and they beheld Elohim and ate and drank. But he already said ״ויראו את אלהי ישראל״ ('they saw the Elohim of Israel'). Rather, it can be interpreted that the nobles beheld Elohim, that is, they prophesied of Him without an intermediary barrier, and lest you say that when it says ויראו it means literal sight, therefore he went back and said ויחזו — prophecy, from the term מחזה ('vision'). And behold, their grasp was above the grasp of Israel and below the grasp of Moses, for the grasp of Israel was through the barrier of cloud and fog, and that of the nobles was prophecy of Him without a barrier, and Moses our master saw Him literally, for so He promised him (Exodus 33): ״וראית את אחורי״ — 'you shall see My back' — and that is the latter glory. And the fact that he immediately mentioned ״ויאכלו וישתו״ indicates that they acted with levity in this vision, and therefore they were worthy of being struck by the hand in the plague, except that He wished to wait for them so as not to mix into the joy of the Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {5 "The reading קרי ביה וישיתו (read it as 'and they drank') introduces a pun: וישתו ('and they drank') is reread as וישיתו from the root עדי/שוא ('to adorn'), linking to the verse cited about עדי ('adornment')."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:57.827129189Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}